WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:01.650
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.800
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.230
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.760
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.480
we will talk about creating
serverless websites

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.370
on Google Cloud.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.560
Imagine you're a
small company that

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.120
got really big, really quickly.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.830
And now, you're seeing a lot of
traffic on your simple website.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.990
But you don't want to deal
with the infrastructure

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.290
setup and maintenance when you
could be focusing on your core

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.230
business.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.930
So let's walk through how not to
worry about the infrastructure

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.480
setup for your website
by deploying it

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.260
on managed serverless
platform like Cloud Run.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.840
If you would like to deploy
on App Engine instead,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.510
there is a link in the
description just for you.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.960
Cloud Run is a managed compute
platform that automatically

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.240
scales stateless containers.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.290
Stateless is important here,
since container instances

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.950
can be started or
stopped at any time.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.060
These containers can be invoked
by web requests or Pub/Sub

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:12.840
events.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.180
Cloud Run is also serverless.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.600
It [INAUDIBLE] all the
infrastructure management

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.640
so you can focus on
what matters most--

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.100
building great applications.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.930
So let's see how to build
a website with Cloud Run.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.480
First, our setup logistics.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.070
Identify or create a GCP project
that you would like to use.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.510
Make sure that the
billing is enabled.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.550
Enable the Cloud Run
and Cloud Build APIs.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.610
If you don't have the
Google Cloud SDK installed,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.780
today's your day.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.570
Go ahead and get that.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.090
OK.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.680
Back to our example.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.870
At the time of this recording,
Cloud Run is in beta,

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.130
so we are going to install the
beta components of the Google

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.880
Cloud SDK using gcloud
components install

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.450
beta command, and then we
will update the components.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.400
This may take a few minutes.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.449
To deploy, you can use any
web app of your choice.

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:11.370
For the purpose of
this demo, we will

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.310
create a hello world web
application in Python

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.410
and then deploy it
using Cloud Run.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:21.810
Create a directory using mkdir
helloworld, and cd into it.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:27.980
Then create a file named app.py
and paste this code into it.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.850
This code creates
a basic web server

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.300
that listens on the ports
defined by port environment

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.150
variable.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.380
Now we are ready to
containerize our app and upload

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.450
to container registry.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.630
For that, we need to
create a new file called

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.890
Dockerfile in the same
directory as the source files.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.220
Here, we define the
official Python image.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.640
Copy the local code to
the container image.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.840
Install production
dependencies and a command

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.810
to run the web service
on container startup.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.200
Now we are ready to build
using Cloud Build by running

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.900
the build command from
the directory containing

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.990
the Dockerfile.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:15.840
Gcloud build submit with
tag gcr.io slash project ID

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.190
slash service name.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.740
I'm just calling it helloworld.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.310
You will see success when
the build is successful.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.950
To deploy the
container image, we now

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:30.930
need to run gcloud beta run
deploy image with the image

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.081
name, gcr.io slash
project ID slash

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:39.260
service name, platform managed.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.540
When prompted to
select the region,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.480
choose 1 for US central1.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.180
Provide a service
name of your choice

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.760
and respond with
the yes to allow

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.110
unauthenticated invocations.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.260
On success, we see
the service URL.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.420
We can visit our
website by opening

00:03:58.420 --> 00:03:59.980
that in our web browser.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.120
Congratulations.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.640
We have just deployed
a web application

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.780
packed in container
image on Cloud Run.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.970
Cloud Run automatically
and horizontally scales

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.600
your container image to handle
the received request, then

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.339
scales back down when
the demand decreases.

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:21.070
You only pay for the CPU,
memory, and networking consumed

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.870
during request handling.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.990
So today, we learned
a second recipe

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.930
for deploying a web
application on Google Cloud.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:33.560
We created and deployed
a dynamic web application

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.430
on Cloud Run and learned that
if you host a website on Cloud

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.860
Run, you can rely on
Google Cloud for it

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.840
to scale with the demand.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.660
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.110
Here's hoping you can
whip up something great.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.200
Join us next time, because we
will share the recipe to host

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.600
websites on compute engine.

